




Phase 3: Requirements Document




Intelligent, AI-Powered Circular Campus Resource Exchange and Asset Life Cycle Management System




 
1. Introduction
1.1 Purpose
The purpose of this document is to record the requirements for the system we are developing. We need a single reference that the team can use when deciding whether a feature belongs in the prototype, and that the instructor can use when assessing whether we have understood the problem and expressed it in a testable way.
We have tried to write statements that can later be checked. Where we are still uncertain we say so, rather than filling the gap with an invented rule. The document is therefore both a specification and a record of the current limits of our analysis.
1.2 Scope
The system is intended for a university environment. Departments frequently hold equipment, furniture or materials that remain unused for long periods while other groups purchase similar items. At the same time, assets pass through a life cycle: they are acquired, used, maintained, sometimes transferred, and eventually donated, recycled or retired. The system we are specifying is meant to support both the internal sharing of idle resources and the tracking of assets over their useful life.
In practical terms the prototype will allow authorised users to register assets, mark them as available for sharing, search for resources (including free-text semantic search), submit and decide requests, record transfers, log maintenance work, and receive limited AI assistance. All AI features run through a local Ollama installation so that data does not need to leave the machines under our control during the course project.
We are not attempting, in this phase, to integrate with the university’s real inventory or identity systems, to build a native mobile application, or to support physical tracking technologies such as RFID or barcodes. We are also not permitting the AI components to change the status of an asset without an explicit human confirmation. These boundaries keep the project feasible for a semester-long team while still allowing us to demonstrate the core ideas required by the brief.
1.3 Definitions
•	Asset — a physical item or reusable resource that the system tracks.
•	Available — a status value meaning that the asset (or a stated quantity of it) may be requested by eligible users.
•	Custodian — the person with day-to-day responsibility for one or more assets.
•	Department Representative — a staff member authorized to manage assets and decide requests on behalf of a department.
•	Life cycle action — one of: keep/reuse, transfer/share, repair, donate, recycle, retire.
•	Llama — an open-source tool that runs language and embedding models on a local machine.
•	Requester — an authenticated user who is allowed to search for and request resources.
•	RBAC — role-based access control.
•	Semantic matching — ranking of search results by meaning, using vector embeddings rather than exact keyword overlap.
