2. Overall Description
2.1 Product Perspective
The system is a new web application built for the course. It is not an extension of an existing commercial product and it is not intended, in its present form, to replace any official university system. In a real institutional deployment one would expect integration with campus identity management and with existing inventory databases. For COSC 336 we treat the application as self-contained: it maintains its own users (or seeded test accounts), its own asset records, its own request workflow and its own local AI services.
The choice to keep the AI components local followed from the feasibility study. Sending asset descriptions and user queries to an external commercial service would raise privacy questions and would also create a dependency on network access and on a third-party key. Running models through Ollama allows us to demonstrate the required AI capabilities while keeping the data on machines we control. The trade-off is a limit on model size; we therefore plan around models in the 7–8 billion parameter range that can run on typical student hardware.
2.2 Summary of Product Functions
At a high level the system must support five groups of activity:
•	Asset management — creating records, updating them, and maintaining a history of significant events.
•	Sharing and discovery — publishing assets as available and allowing other users to find them.
•	Request and approval — a controlled workflow so that a requester can ask for an item and the owning department can accept or refuse.
•	Transfer and maintenance — recording the physical handover and any inspection or repair work.
•	AI assistance — classification help at registration, semantic ranking of search results, life cycle suggestions, a natural-language helper, and short narrative reports. All of these run through the local Ollama instance.
The basic path from registration through to transfer must remain usable even if the AI components are slow or temporarily unavailable. That requirement influenced the way several of the use cases were written.
2.3 User Characteristics
We expect the majority of users to be university staff. In some departments senior students or research assistants may also act as requester. The typical user is comfortable with ordinary web forms and short notifications. We do not assume any background in machine learning or database administration.
Administrators will need a slightly higher level of familiarity because they manage roles and may handle escalated requests. Even for them the interface should remain straightforward. During development and demonstration we will rely on a small set of seeded accounts that cover the different roles, so that complete scenarios can be walked through without a full user-management subsystem.
2.4 Operating Environment
The prototype is intended to run on ordinary student and laboratory machines. The back-end will be a Python process using Fast API, the database will be PostgreSQL (or a compatible engine for local development), and the front-end will be a React application built with Vite. Ollama will run as a separate local process that the back-end contacts over HTTP on localhost.
Mobile phones are not treated as a primary platform, although the interface should remain usable on a tablet. Issues such as load balancing, HTTPS termination and container orchestration lie outside the scope of the course project.


2.5 Design and Implementation Constraints
Several practical limits shape what we can deliver within the course:
•	The remaining time in the semester and the capacity of a student team.
•	The decision to keep AI processing local (no external commercial LLM API’s for the core features).
•	Hardware limits on student laptops; models larger than roughly 8B parameters become difficult to run, so we target llama3.1:8b or qwen2.5:7b for generation and nomic-embed-text for embeddings.
•	The possible unavailability of real university asset data; sample or synthetic data will be used unless formal permission is obtained.
•	The team’s greater familiarity with Python and JavaScript, which supports the choice of Fast API and React.
2.6 Assumptions and Dependencies
We assume that every team member can install Ollama and pull at least one suitable chat model and one embedding model, that PostgreSQL (or SQLite for early work) is available, and that the front-end, back-end and Ollama process can communicate on the local machine without unusual permission problems. We also assume that feedback on sample workflows can be obtained even if real institutional data is not supplied. If any of these assumptions proves false we will need to simplify the AI features or move to smaller models.
3. Functional Requirements
The requirements in this section describe the services the system must provide. They are grouped by area and given identifiers so that they can be traced later to use cases, design elements and tests.
3.1 Asset and Resource Management
•	FR-01: An authorized user shall be able to register a new asset. The record must include at least a unique identifier, a short description, a category, a condition rating, a quantity, a location and the owning department.
•	FR-02: An authorized user shall be able to update the details of an existing asset, including changes to condition and location.
•	FR-03: A department representative or custodian shall be able to mark an asset (or a stated quantity of it) as available for sharing or transfer.
•	FR-04: The system shall maintain a chronological history of significant events for each asset. As a minimum the history must cover registration, status changes, transfers, maintenance entries, donation, recycling and retirement.
•	FR-05: An authorized user shall be able to unpublish an asset so that it no longer appears in search results for new requests, while the asset record itself is retained.
A reliable register and history are the foundation for everything else. Without them the sharing and lifecycle features have nothing solid to operate on. The history is also required later for the AI lifecycle suggestions.
3.2 Search, Request and Approval
•	FR-06: A requester shall be able to search available resources using keywords, category, location, condition and free-text descriptions.
•	FR-07: A requester shall be able to submit a short-term reservation or a formal transfer request against an available asset.
•	FR-08: The system shall route each request to the appropriate department representative or administrator for a decision.
•	FR-09: The system shall notify the requester of the outcome of a request. When the request is approved, the system shall support the subsequent recording of the transfer.
•	FR-10: The system shall refuse the creation of a request when the requested quantity exceeds the quantity currently marked as available.
A search limited to exact keywords would be too weak for practical use. Free-text matching, supported by local embeddings, makes the catalogue more useful. The approval step exists because departments need to retain control over their own equipment.
3.3 Maintenance and Lifecycle Closure
•	FR-11: Maintenance staff or an authorized custodian shall be able to record inspection, repair or service work against an asset, including the date, a description of the work and an optional updated condition.
•	FR-12: Authorized staff shall be able to record that an asset has been donated, recycled or retired, and to close its active life cycle.
Maintenance history improves later decisions about whether an item is worth repairing. Closing the life cycle cleanly prevents retired items from continuing to appear as available.
3.4 AI-Supported Features
The following features all depend on a local Ollama installation. They are assistive only; none of them is allowed to change the status of an asset without an explicit human action.
•	FR-13: When a user supplies a free-text description of a need, the system shall return a ranked list of candidate resources by combining local embeddings with hard filters for availability, quantity, condition and location.
•	FR-14: When a new asset is being registered, the system shall be able to suggest a category, a set of tags and a cleaned description. The user must be able to accept, edit or discard the suggestion.
•	FR-15: For an existing asset the system shall be able to suggest a next life-cycle action chosen from the set {reuse/keep, transfer/share, repair, donate, recycle, retire}, based on the recorded condition and history. The suggestion is advisory only.
•	FR-16: The system shall provide a natural-language assistant that can answer questions the user is permitted to ask, start a search, or begin a request, while always respecting the user’s role and permissions.
•	FR-17: The system shall be able to generate a short narrative report from real database aggregates. The underlying numbers must come from queries; the language model is used only to turn those numbers into readable prose.
These five capabilities correspond to the AI features required by the project brief. Keeping them local addresses the privacy and cost concerns identified in the feasibility study.
3.5 Users, Access Control and Audit
•	FR-18: The system shall authenticate users and enforce role-based access control. The roles used in the prototype are Administrator, Department Representative, Custodian, Requester and Maintenance Staff.
•	FR-19: The system shall record, for every significant action, which user performed the action and when it occurred.
Role-based access is simpler to reason about than per-user permissions for a course project. Audit records are needed both for accountability and for later investigation of the workflow.
4. Non-Functional Requirements
Non-functional requirements describe qualities of the system and constraints on how it is built or operated. They do not specify a particular feature, but they influence almost every design decision.
4.1 Performance and Availability of Core Functions
•	NFR-01: The core path (register an asset, search, submit a request, approve or reject, record a transfer) shall remain fully usable even when the local Ollama process is stopped or is responding slowly.
•	NFR-02: Typical interactive AI operations (classification suggestion, semantic ranking of a moderate result set, a short assistant reply) should complete within approximately 10–15 seconds on a machine that can run a 7–8B model. Longer times are acceptable for report generation.
•	NFR-03: The web interface shall remain responsive under the light concurrent load expected during a course demonstration.
We deliberately made the core workflow independent of the AI components so that a slow or missing model does not freeze the application. The response-time target is a compromise between model quality and the patience of a user working on a laptop.
4.2 Technology and Development Constraints
•	NFR-04: The prototype shall be implemented using React with Vite for the front-end, FastAPI for the back-end, and PostgreSQL (or a compatible database) for persistence.
•	NFR-05: AI features shall be provided by a local Ollama instance. The preferred generation models are llama3.1:8b or qwen2.5:7b; the preferred embedding model is nomic-embed-text. A smaller model may be documented as a fallback for low-RAM machines.
•	NFR-06: Development and demonstration shall use sample or synthetic data unless the team obtains formal written permission to use real university records.
4.3 Security and Privacy
•	NFR-07: Access to asset records, requests and reports shall be restricted according to the user’s role and, where applicable, the user’s department.
•	NFR-08: All AI inference for the prototype shall run locally via Ollama. Prompts and asset data shall not be sent to external network services as part of normal operation.
•	NFR-09: Every significant state-changing action shall leave an audit record containing at least the actor identifier and a timestamp.
•	NFR-10: User credentials shall not be stored in plain text.
The local-AI decision is the strongest privacy measure we can take inside the course constraints. Role-based access and audit logging are the minimum controls needed to demonstrate responsible handling of shared resources.
4.4 Usability
•	NFR-11: A user who belongs to a given role should be able to reach the most common actions for that role within a small number of navigation steps from the main screen.
•	NFR-12: Error messages shown to end users shall be written in ordinary language and, where possible, shall suggest how to correct the problem.
•	NFR-13: The interface shall work in current versions of the major desktop browsers without requiring additional plugins.
4.5 Reliability
•	NFR-14: A failure of the Ollama process shall not corrupt asset or request data. The user shall be informed that AI assistance is temporarily unavailable and shall be able to continue with non-AI actions.
•	NFR-15: The system shall not lose a successfully submitted request or a successfully recorded transfer because of a transient error that is later retried.





5. Use Case Diagram and Detailed Use Cases
This section follows the Phase 3 guidance. It presents one system-level use-case diagram and then a set of textual use cases. Each use case describes a goal that an actor wants to achieve and the sequence of steps that lead to that goal or to a controlled failure.
5.1 Actors
We identified five actors. In a larger institutional system there might be more specialized roles, but for the prototype these five cover the interactions we need.
•	Requester — any authenticated user who needs a resource and is allowed to search and submit requests. In practice this will often be a staff member or a senior student working under supervision.
•	Department Representative — the person who manages a department’s assets from the system’s point of view. They publish resources, review incoming requests and may record transfers.
•	Custodian — someone closer to the physical items (for example a laboratory technician). They can register assets, update condition, publish availability and log maintenance.
•	Maintenance Staff — users whose main task in the system is to record inspection and repair work. They may or may not also hold a custodian role.
•	Administrator — a small number of users who manage accounts and roles, and who can step in for cross-department or escalated decisions.
An actor is not necessarily a single human being. In principle another system could play the role of a requester, but in the current prototype all actors are human users working through the web interface.
 
