5.3 Detailed Use Cases
Each use case below follows the structure illustrated in the Phase 3 slides: name, actors, preconditions, trigger, main flow, alternative flows, exception flows and post conditions. A short paragraph of explanation is added after some of the use cases where the reasoning behind a decision may not be obvious from the steps alone.
UC-01: Register Asset
Actors: Department Representative or Custodian (primary). An Administrator may also register assets if needed.
Preconditions: The actor is authenticated and has permission to register assets for the relevant department. A unique identifier is either supplied by the user or generated by the system.
Trigger: The actor selects the Register Asset action from the asset-management area of the interface.
Main Flow:
1.	The actor opens the registration form.
2.	The system presents empty fields for identifier, title or short description, category, condition, quantity, location, owning department and optional free-text notes.
3.	The actor fills in the information that is already known. If help with classification is wanted, the actor may request an AI suggestion.
4.	If an AI suggestion is requested, the system sends the free-text description to the local Ollama model and receives a proposed category, a set of tags and a cleaned description.
5.	The actor accepts the suggestion as it stands, edits any part of it, or discards it and continues with manually entered values.
6.	The actor submits the completed form.
7.	The system validates that all mandatory fields are present and that the identifier is unique within the system.
8.	The system creates the asset record and writes an initial history entry that records who registered the asset and when.
9.	The system displays a confirmation message that includes the new asset identifier.
Alternative Flows:
•	If the actor never requests an AI suggestion, steps 4 and 5 are skipped and the flow continues from step 6 with purely manual data.
•	If the Ollama service does not respond within an acceptable time, the system informs the actor that AI assistance is temporarily unavailable and allows the registration to continue with manual entry.
Exception Flows:
•	If validation fails (a required field is empty or the identifier already exists), the system highlights the problem fields, retains the data the actor has already typed, and returns the actor to the form so that corrections can be made.
Postconditions:
•	A new asset record exists in the database.
•	An initial history entry has been stored.
•	The asset is not yet visible to requesters; visibility requires a separate publish action (UC-02).
We kept the AI suggestion optional and easy to override. Classification models are helpful for consistency of language, but they are not always correct for specialised laboratory equipment. Leaving the final choice with the human user reduces the risk that systematically wrong categories will spread through the catalogue.
 
UC-02: Publish Available Resource
Actors: Department Representative or Custodian (primary).
Preconditions: The asset already exists in the system and is not in a terminal state (retired, donated or recycled). The actor has permission to change the availability of assets belonging to that department.
Trigger: The actor selects an asset and chooses the Publish or Mark Available action.
Main Flow:
10.	The actor selects the target asset from a list or from a previous search.
11.	The system displays the current status of the asset and a short form that lets the actor set the available quantity and add any usage notes or restrictions.
12.	The actor confirms the quantity and any notes.
13.	The system updates the availability status of the asset and appends a history entry that records the publication.
14.	The system confirms that the resource is now visible to users who are permitted to see it.
Alternative Flows:
•	The actor may choose to publish only a portion of the total quantity. In that case the system stores the available quantity separately from the total quantity owned by the department.
Exception Flows:
•	If the asset is already published, or if it has already been moved to a terminal lifecycle state, the system informs the actor and does not alter any data.
Postconditions:
•	The asset (or the stated quantity) can appear in search results for eligible requesters.
•	A history entry records the publication event.
Publishing is intentionally a separate step from registration. Not every registered asset should automatically be offered for sharing. Some items are intended for internal use only, and some are still being prepared or repaired. Keeping the two actions distinct gives the department control over what is visible to the rest of the campus.
 
UC-03: Search Resources
Actors: Requester (primary). Department Representatives and Custodians may also search, for example to check what they have already published.
Preconditions: The actor is authenticated. At least one asset has been published as available; otherwise the result list will simply be empty.
Trigger: The actor opens the search page or submits a search query from another part of the interface.
Main Flow:
15.	The actor enters search criteria. These may include structured filters (category, location, condition) and/or a free-text description of the need.
16.	The system identifies the set of assets that are currently marked available and that the actor is authorized to view.
17.	If a free-text description was supplied, the system obtains a vector embedding of the query from the local embedding model, ranks the candidate assets by similarity, and then applies the hard filters for availability, quantity, condition and location.
18.	The system presents the resulting list. Each entry shows enough information (title, category, condition, location, available quantity and a short description) for the actor to decide whether to examine the item further or to start a request.
19.	The actor may select any result to view full details or to begin the Request Resource use case (UC-04).
Alternative Flows:
•	If the actor supplies only structured filters and no free-text, the system skips the embedding step and returns a filtered list ordered by a simple default such as most recently published.
•	If the embedding service is unavailable, the system falls back to ordinary keyword matching against title and description fields and displays a notice that semantic ranking is temporarily turned off.
Exception Flows:
•	If the search produces no results, the system states this clearly and may offer simple suggestions such as removing a filter or broadening the free-text query.
Postconditions:
•	The actor has been shown a list of candidate resources (which may be empty).
•	No request has been created; that requires a separate use case.
Making the semantic ranking an enhancement rather than a hard dependency is important. On a laptop with limited resources the embedding step can take a few seconds. Users should still be able to locate resources with ordinary filters if the model is slow or missing.
 
UC-04: Request Resource
Actors: Requester (primary). The Department Representative or Administrator becomes involved later when the request is decided.
Preconditions: The actor is authenticated and has the Requester role (or a higher role that includes requesting). The target asset is currently marked available and has sufficient remaining quantity.
Trigger: The actor selects an available asset and chooses Request or Reserve.
Main Flow:
20.	The actor views the asset details and starts a request.
21.	The system presents a short form asking for the request type (reservation or transfer), the quantity, the desired period or start date, and an optional justification.
22.	The actor completes the form and submits it.
23.	The system re-checks that the asset is still available and that the requested quantity does not exceed the available quantity.
24.	The system creates a request record with status Pending and writes an audit entry.
25.	The system notifies the responsible Department Representative (and the Administrator if an escalation rule applies).
26.	The system shows the requester a confirmation page that includes the request identifier.
Alternative Flows:
•	If the actor abandons the form before submitting, no request is stored and the use case ends.
Exception Flows:
•	If between the moment the actor opened the form and the moment of submission the asset became unavailable, the system refuses to create the request and explains the reason.
•	If the requested quantity is higher than the available quantity, the system shows the current available number and asks the actor to reduce the request.
Postconditions:
•	A Pending request exists in the system.
•	The decision-maker has been notified.
•	The requester has received a confirmation containing the request identifier.
We discussed whether a Pending request should automatically reduce the visible available quantity. For the prototype we lean toward a temporary reservation, but the exact rule can be adjusted during implementation. The important point is that two people should not both receive approval for the last remaining unit of an item.
 
UC-05: Approve or Reject Request
Actors: Department Representative or Administrator (primary). The original Requester receives the outcome.
Preconditions: A request with status Pending exists. The actor has permission to decide requests for the owning department (or holds Administrator privileges).
Trigger: The actor opens a pending request from the approval queue or from a notification.
Main Flow:
27.	The actor reviews the request details, the current state of the asset, and any justification supplied by the requester.
28.	The actor chooses Approve or Reject and may type a short comment.
29.	The system records the decision, the identity of the actor, the timestamp and the optional comment.
30.	The system updates the status of the request.
31.	The system notifies the original requester of the outcome.
32.	If the decision was Approve, the system indicates that the transfer can now be recorded (UC-06).
Alternative Flows:
•	The actor may request more information instead of deciding immediately. In that case the request remains Pending, a comment is stored, and the requester is notified.
Exception Flows:
•	If at the moment of decision the asset is no longer available, the system warns the actor and prevents approval until the conflict is resolved.
Postconditions:
•	The request status is either Approved or Rejected.
•	An audit entry for the decision exists.
•	The requester has been informed of the outcome.
Keeping the decision with a human is intentional. Even if a later version of the system offered an AI recommendation on whether to approve, the actual approve or reject action should remain with a person who understands the department’s current priorities and constraints.
 
UC-06: Record Transfer
Actors: Department Representative, Custodian or Administrator (primary).
Preconditions: There is an approved request linked to the asset. The actor has permission to record transfers.
Trigger: The actor selects an approved request and chooses Record Transfer.
Main Flow:
33.	The actor confirms the asset, the receiving person or department, and the date of the handover.
34.	The actor may add a short note about the physical condition at handover or any accessories that were included.
35.	The actor confirms the transfer.
36.	The system updates the asset’s ownership or location fields and adjusts the available quantity.
37.	The system appends a history entry describing the transfer.
38.	The system sets the request status to Completed.
39.	The system displays a confirmation to the actor.
Exception Flows:
•	If mandatory confirmation data is missing, the system asks for the missing pieces before it will save the transfer.
Postconditions:
•	The asset record reflects the new ownership or location.
•	The history contains a transfer event.
•	The related request is marked Completed.
In a real campus setting the physical handover might involve a paper form or a signature. For the prototype we only record that the transfer was acknowledged inside the system. Adding digital signatures or photographic evidence is a possible later enhancement, but it is not required for the course demonstration.
 
UC-07: Log Maintenance
Actors: Maintenance Staff or Custodian (primary).
Preconditions: The asset exists. The actor has permission to add maintenance records for it.
Trigger: The actor selects an asset and chooses Log Maintenance.
Main Flow:
40.	The actor opens the maintenance form for the chosen asset.
41.	The actor enters the type of work (inspection, repair, routine service, or other), the date, a free-text description, and optionally a new condition rating.
42.	The actor submits the form.
43.	The system checks that the required fields are present.
44.	The system stores the maintenance record and links it to the asset’s history.
45.	If a new condition rating was supplied, the system updates the condition field on the asset.
46.	The system confirms that the entry was saved.
Exception Flows:
•	If validation fails, the system indicates which fields are missing or invalid and lets the actor correct them.
Postconditions:
•	A maintenance record exists and is visible in the asset’s history.
•	The asset’s condition field reflects the latest supplied rating, if any.
Maintenance data is useful both for human decision makers and for the AI lifecycle-suggestion feature. Even short free-text notes help later when someone is trying to decide whether an item is still worth repairing or should be retired.
 
UC-08: Suggest Lifecycle Action
Actors: Department Representative, Custodian or Administrator (primary).
Preconditions: The asset exists and has at least a condition value and some history. The actor is allowed to view the asset. The local Ollama service is reachable; otherwise the use case reports unavailability.
Trigger: The actor opens an asset and selects Suggest next action or an equivalent command.
Main Flow:
47.	The system collects a compact summary of the asset: category, current condition, age or key history points, current status, and recent maintenance.
48.	The system sends that summary to the local chat model with instructions to return one action from the allowed set {reuse/keep, transfer/share, repair, donate, recycle, retire} together with a short justification.
49.	The system receives the model’s reply and displays the suggested action and the reason, together with a clear statement that the suggestion is only advice.
50.	The actor may ignore the suggestion or may navigate to the appropriate update, publish or lifecycle-closure action if they decide to follow it.
Alternative Flows:
•	If the model returns an action outside the allowed set, or returns text that cannot be parsed reliably, the system discards the reply and tells the actor that a reliable suggestion could not be produced.
Exception Flows:
•	If the asset has almost no history or condition data, the system informs the actor that more information is needed before a suggestion can be offered.
•	If Ollama does not respond, the system informs the actor that the suggestion service is temporarily unavailable. All other asset operations remain open.
Postconditions:
•	Either a suggestion has been shown to the actor, or a clear unavailability or insufficient-data message has been shown.
•	No change has been made to the asset’s status solely because of the suggestion.
This use case is the clearest illustration of the principle we adopted in the feasibility study: the AI may recommend, but a human decides. The allowed action vocabulary is kept small and closed so that the model cannot invent statuses that the rest of the system does not understand.
 
UC-09: Generate Report
Actors: Administrator or Department Representative (primary).
Preconditions: The actor has permission to view the metrics for the chosen scope (whole system or a single department). Enough transactional data exists to compute at least some totals.
Trigger: The actor chooses Generate Report, selects a report type and a time range, and confirms.
Main Flow:
51.	The system runs the database queries that calculate the required aggregates (for example number of completed transfers, number of maintenance events, number of assets published, or rough estimates of avoided purchases if such figures are stored).
52.	The system constructs a prompt that contains only the calculated numbers and a clear instruction to produce a short, neutral narrative that does not introduce any figures that were not supplied.
53.	The system sends the prompt to the local chat model and receives the generated text.
54.	The system displays the narrative and the raw numbers together so that the actor can see the basis of the text.
55.	The actor may copy the text or export it for use in a meeting or a progress report.
Alternative Flows:
•	If the language model is unavailable, the system still shows the raw metrics and adds a note that the narrative could not be generated. The use case is still considered successful with respect to data presentation.
Exception Flows:
•	If the chosen period contains no relevant records, the system tells the actor that there is nothing to report and does not call the language model.
Postconditions:
•	The actor has been shown a set of figures that originate from the database, optionally accompanied by a narrative that does not invent extra numbers.
The strict separation between numbers taken from the database and wording produced by the model is deliberate. It makes the report feature safer to demonstrate and easier to defend if someone asks whether the AI is inventing statistics.
6. Data Considerations
At the requirements level we do not need a full database schema, but we do need to be clear about the main concepts the system must remember. The following conceptual entities are required to support the use cases described above.
•	User — identity, authentication credentials (stored safely), role or roles, and department affiliation where relevant.
•	Asset — unique identifier, description, category, condition, total quantity, available quantity, location, owning department, current status, and timestamps for creation and last update.
•	AssetHistory — a sequence of events linked to an asset. Each event has a type, an actor, a timestamp, and optional structured or free-text details.
•	Request — link to the requester, link to the asset, requested quantity, request type, status (Pending / Approved / Rejected / Completed), justification, decision comment, and timestamps.
•	MaintenanceRecord — link to the asset, type of work, date, description, optional new condition, and the actor who logged it.
We prefer soft status changes over hard deletion. Once an asset has history, removing the row entirely would destroy information that later lifecycle or reporting features need. The same idea applies to requests: a rejected or completed request should remain in the database for audit purposes.
For the AI search feature we will also need to store or compute embeddings of asset descriptions. Whether those vectors live in the main PostgreSQL database or in a lightweight side store is a design decision; the requirement is simply that the search use case can obtain them when needed.
7. User Interface Considerations
The Phase 3 guidance does not require a full set of screen mock-ups, but it is still useful to state the main expectations so that the later design phase has a starting point.
The interface will be a web application. After login the user should see a simple home view whose content depends on their role. A Requester should see search and “my requests” prominently. A Department Representative should see pending approvals and a shortcut to their department’s assets. An Administrator should see user-management functions and system-level reports.
Forms should be short and should use ordinary labels. When the system can offer an AI suggestion (for example during registration), the suggestion should appear as a clearly marked optional aid, never as a mandatory step. Error messages should be written in plain language. For example, “This identifier is already used by another asset” is preferable to a raw database constraint error.
Because we are not targeting mobile phones as a primary platform, the layout can assume a reasonable desktop or laptop screen width. Responsive behaviour that keeps the application usable on a tablet is desirable but is not a top priority for the first prototype.
8. Local AI Features – Further Discussion
The project brief requires five AI-related capabilities. In the feasibility study we decided that all of them would run through a local Ollama instance. This section gathers the main points in one place.
8.1 Why Local
Three considerations led us to a local-only approach. First, privacy: asset descriptions and user queries may contain information that the university would prefer not to send to an external commercial service. Second, cost and dependency: a student project should not rely on a paid API key that might expire or whose terms might change. Third, demonstrability: we can show the complete system on a laptop without needing internet access for the AI part.
The price of that decision is a limit on model size. On a machine with 16 GB of RAM we can run 7–8B parameter models with acceptable speed. Larger models become slow or fail to load. That is why the preferred generation models are llama3.1:8b and qwen2.5:7b, and why the preferred embedding model is nomic-embed-text, which is small and effective for retrieval tasks.
8.2 Mapping of AI Features to Use Cases
•	Semantic resource matching is used inside UC-03.
•	Asset classification is used inside UC-01.
•	Lifecycle suggestion is the whole of UC-08.
•	The natural-language assistant is a cross-cutting capability covered by FR-16; its detailed interaction design will be worked out in the design phase.
•	Generative reporting is the whole of UC-09.
In every case the AI output is either a suggestion that the user can edit or a narrative that is explicitly grounded in database numbers. No AI output is permitted to change the status of an asset by itself.
8.3 Behaviour when the Model is Unavailable
If Ollama is not running, or if a request to it times out, the system must degrade gracefully. Search falls back to keyword matching. Registration continues with manual entry. Lifecycle suggestion and narrative report generation simply inform the user that the service is unavailable. The core register–request–approve–transfer path never blocks on the language model.
9. Open Issues and Traceability
9.1 Open Issues
A few decisions remain open. They do not prevent design from starting, but they should be settled explicitly rather than left to individual implementers.
•	Asset identifier format — should identifiers be system-generated UUIDs, or should departments be allowed to supply their own codes (for example “CHEM-2024-017”)?
•	Reservation versus availability — when a request is created with status Pending, should the available quantity be reduced immediately, or only after approval?
•	Escalation rules — under what conditions (asset value, quantity, cross-department transfer) must an Administrator approve in addition to the Department Representative?
•	Retention period — how long should completed requests and detailed history entries be kept? For the prototype we can keep everything; a real system would need a retention policy.
•	Scope of the assistant — is the natural-language assistant part of the first vertical slice that we implement, or is it deferred until the basic workflow is stable?
We will resolve these points during the design phase and will update this document if any resolution changes the observable behaviour of the system.
9.2 Traceability Notes
Each functional requirement listed in Section 3 is referenced from one or more use cases in Section 5 through the natural language of the flows and the postconditions. Non-functional requirements are satisfied mainly by architectural choices and by tests rather than by individual use-case steps. The identifiers (FR-xx, NFR-xx, UC-xx) are sufficient to support discussion and review. A more formal traceability matrix can be produced later if the instructor requests it.
10. Glossary
•	Actor — a role played by a user (or another system) when interacting with the system under design.
•	Alternative flow — a variation of the main success scenario that still leads to a successful outcome.
•	Embedding — a numeric vector that represents the meaning of a piece of text; used for semantic similarity search.
•	Exception flow — a path that handles an error or unexpected condition.
•	Functional requirement — a statement of a service or behaviour the system must provide.
•	Main flow (basic path) — the normal sequence of steps that achieves the use-case goal.
•	Non-functional requirement — a constraint on the system or a quality it must possess (performance, security, usability, etc.).
•	Postcondition — a condition that is true after a use case has finished (successfully or otherwise).
•	Precondition — a condition that must be true before a use case can start.
•	Primary actor — the actor who initiates the use case in order to achieve a goal.
•	Secondary actor — an actor who participates in the use case but does not initiate it.
•	Trigger — the event that causes a use case to begin.
•	Use case — a description of how an actor interacts with the system to achieve a goal.
•	Use case diagram — a diagram that shows actors, use cases and the relationships between them.
11. Closing Remarks
This requirements document has tried to describe the Circular Campus Resource Exchange and Asset Life Cycle Management System in enough detail that design and implementation can begin with a shared understanding of what is required. We have kept the AI features firmly in an assistive role and have insisted that the basic sharing workflow remain usable even when the local language models are not available.
The document is a living artefact. If, during design or early implementation, we discover that a requirement is unclear, incomplete or inconsistent, we will update the document and note the change. For the purposes of the Phase 3 submission we believe the current set of requirements, the use-case diagram and the detailed use cases provide a solid foundation for the work that follows.
